WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.560
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.100
This will be a little non-traditional video
where the first half of the episode will be

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.850
about a paper, and the second part will be
about…something else.

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:15.280
Also a paper.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.580
Well, kind of.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:17.580
You’ll see.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:22.199
We’ve seen in the previous years that neural
network-based learning methods are amazing

00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:27.079
at image classification, which means that
after training on a few thousand training

00:00:27.079 --> 00:00:32.520
examples, they can look at a new, previously
unseen image and tell us whether it depicts

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.810
a frog or a bus.

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:40.060
Earlier we have shown that we can fool neural
networks by adding carefully crafted noise

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:45.440
to an image, which we often refer to as an
adversarial attack on a neural network.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:52.000
If done well, this noise is barely perceptible
and, get this, can fool the classifier into

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.610
looking at a bus and thinking that it is an
ostrich.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:01.560
These attacks typically require modifying
a large portion of the input image, so when

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:07.930
talking about a later paper, we were thinking,
what could be the lowest number of pixel changes

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:11.170
that we have to perform to fool a neural network?

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.170
What is the magic number?

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:17.830
Based on the results of previous research
works, an educated guess would be somewhere

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:19.220
around a hundred pixels.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:26.160
A followup paper gave us an unbelievable answer
by demonstrating the one pixel attack.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:32.050
You see here that by changing only one pixel
in an image that depicts a horse, the AI will

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:36.200
be 99.9% sure that we are seeing a frog.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:43.310
A ship can also be disguised as a car, or,
amusingly, with a properly executed one-pixel

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:48.350
attack, almost anything can be seen as an
airplane by the neural network.

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:53.630
And, this new paper discusses whether we should
look at these adversarial examples as bugs

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:57.400
or not, and of course, does a lot more than
that!

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:02.720
It argues that most datasets contain features
that are predictive, meaning that they provide

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:08.910
help for a classifier to find cats, but also
non-robust, which means that they provide

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:14.819
a rather brittle understanding that falls
apart in the presence adversarial changes.

00:02:14.819 --> 00:02:20.550
We are also shown how to find and eliminate
these non-robust features from already existing

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:26.330
datasets and that we can build much more robust
classifier neural networks as a result.

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:31.040
This is a truly excellent paper that sparked
quite a bit of discussion.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:34.940
And here comes the second part of the video
with the something else.

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:39.560
An interesting new article was published within
the Distill journal, a journal where you can

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:45.030
expect clearly worded papers with beautiful
and interactive visualizations.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:50.330
But this is no ordinary article, this is a
so-called discussion article where a number

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:55.120
of researchers were asked to write comments
on this paper and create interesting back

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.360
and forth discussions with the original authors.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:03.840
Now, make no mistake, the paper we’ve talked
about was peer-reviewed, which means that

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:10.220
independent experts have spent time scrutinizing
the validity of the results, so this new discussion

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:15.319
article was meant to add to it by getting
others to replicate the results and clear

00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:17.690
up potential misunderstandings.

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:22.470
Through publishing six of these mini-discussions,
each of which were addressed by the original

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:29.040
authors, they were able to clarify the main
takeaways of the paper, and even added a section

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.760
of non-claims as well.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:36.330
For instance, it’s been clarified that they
don’t claim that adversarial examples arise

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.599
from software bugs.

00:03:37.599 --> 00:03:43.400
A huge thanks to the Distill journal and all
the authors who participated in this discussion,

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:47.790
and Ferenc Huszár, who suggested the idea
of the discussion article to the journal.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:52.210
I’d love to see more of this, and if you
do too, make sure to leave a comment so we

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:57.390
can show them that these endeavors to raise
the replicability and clarity of research

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:59.490
works are indeed welcome.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:03.360
Make sure to click the link to both works
in the video description, and spend a little

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:04.660
quality time with them.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:06.220
You’ll be glad you did.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:11.470
I think this was a more complex than average
paper to talk about, however, as you have

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:14.480
noticed, the usual visual fireworks were not
there.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:18.130
As a result, I expect this to get significantly
fewer views.

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:23.160
That’s not a great business model, but no
matter, I made this channel so I can share

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:27.699
with you all these important lessons that
I learned during my journey.

00:04:27.699 --> 00:04:32.310
This has been a true privilege and I am thrilled
that I am still able to talk about all these

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:38.350
amazing papers without worrying too much whether
any of these videos will go viral or not.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:44.729
Videos like this one are only possible because
of your support on Patreon.com/TwoMinutePapers.

00:04:44.729 --> 00:04:49.210
If you feel like chipping in, just click the
Patreon link in the video description.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:52.960
This is why every video ends with, you know
what’s coming…

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.380
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

